From 9a523b78b87eca0152773b2d85ad3155f55af16f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OnePlus 11 mainline port <noreply@localhost>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 13:47:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: drive OnePlus SM8550
 SUPERVOOC from the host

On salami the ADSP runs SUPERVOOC (stock oplus,adsp-voocphy,
DUAL_BATT_100W): it talks to the adapter and owns the current tables and
the battery, BTB and overvoltage protection. The stock host enables it
on a DCP, keeps the PMIC charger off while it charges, restarts a
stalled handshake once and guards the USB connector temperature.
Without that host side the ADSP never starts: voocphy reads back 0 and a
replug of the stock charger only raises notification 0x64.

Add that host side for boards whose pmic-glink node names the connector
thermistors (usb_l, usb_r), behind a vooc module parameter that
defaults to off; with it off the driver asks the firmware nothing new.
When allowed, every 5 s while a charger is attached it enables voocphy
only on a DCP below the charge limit, with the battery at 12.0-43.0 C
and both connector readings valid, under 50 C and less than 12 C above
the battery, all tighter than stock. On 0x52 it suspends the PMIC
charger and gives the handshake 8.5 s, retrying once; 0x48 status
codes resume the PMIC charger when the fast path ends, and errors,
hardware limits, a hot connector or a restarted ADSP keep fast charging
off until the next plug. Unloading leaves the ADSP on its own charger.
0x64 drives host charge pumps that salami does not have, so it is now
ignored. The salami DTS adds the channels with post-init-providers so
pmic_glink does not wait for the ADC.

Tested on salami from USB to a PC (SDP), no fast charger: with vooc=0
nothing changes; with vooc=1 the driver reads voocphy 0 and the
connector at 25.2/25.3 C every 5 s and keeps it off as not a DCP. The
DCP path, the handshake and the PMIC charger suspend are untested.

